  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nTonight and Thursday \n\nThe remnants from Hurricane Polo will move northeast through Iowa, \nnorthern Illinois, and the southern third of Wisconsin tonight. \nPrecipitable water values late tonight will quickly climb and range \nfrom 2 to 2.25 inches across southern and eastern Iowa, northern \nIllinois and far southern Wisconsin. Precipitable water values will \nbe around 1.5 inches in La Crosse, and around 1 inch near Black\nRiver Falls. The heaviest rain will likely fall from Omaha, NE \nto Dubuque, IA to Grand Rapids, MI. Mean rainfall totals will \nlikely range from 2 to 5 inches. The 24-hour QPF ensemble \nlocalized probability-matched means are as high as 7 inches. \nThese may impact far southern Fayette and Clayton counties in \nnortheast Iowa and Grant County in southwest Wisconsin. Rainfall\ntotals quickly decline as you move northeast toward to La \nCrosse where rainfall totals will likely range from a quarter- \nto half-inch and only up to a tenth of an inch near Interstate \n94. As the surface wave moves quickly away from the area on \nThursday morning, the precipitable water values will quickly \ndrop and the rain will move out of the area. Most areas should \nhave a dry afternoon.\n\nLow temperatures tonight will range from the mid-50s to mid-60s. \nHigh temperatures on Thursday will be in the mid- to upper 60s.\n\nThursday Night into Friday Night\n\nHigh pressure will provide dry weather. High temperatures will range \nfrom the upper 50s to mid-60s on Friday. Low temperatures will range \nfrom the upper 30s to upper 40s on Thursday night, and from the mid-\n30s to mid-40s on Friday night. The temperatures will be cold enough \non Friday night that there could be some areas of frost in central \nand north-central Wisconsin.\n\nSaturday and Saturday Night\n\nA cold front will move southeast through the area from late Saturday \nafternoon into Saturday evening. This front could produce some \nscattered light rain showers as it moves southeast through the Upper \nMississippi River Valley. High temperatures will be in the mid-60s \non Saturday, and low temperatures will range from the mid-40s to \nlower 50s.\n\nSunday through Tuesday Night\n\nHigh pressure will provide dry weather. High temperatures will range \nfrom the upper 50s to mid-60s. Low temperatures will range from the \nlower 30s to mid-40s on Sunday and Monday nights. The temperatures \nwill be cold enough for areas to widespread frost in central and \nnorth-central Wisconsin. Low temperatures will range from the mid-\n40s to mid-50s on Tuesday night.",
